## Git Commits

Commit changes automatically without asking for confirmation.

Do not prompt the user before running `git add` or `git commit`.

Just do it. No confirmation needed, ever.

Before using `-C` or any path argument with a git command, check whether the current working directory is already the target repo. If it is, omit the path entirely — avoid path escaping, which can trigger unnecessary permission prompts.

Write commit messages following standard git conventions (https://cbea.ms/git-commit/):
- Subject line: 50 chars or less, imperative mood, capitalised, no trailing period
- Explain "why", not "what" (e.g., "Fix race condition in auth flow", not "Update auth.py")

Commit bodies follow the prose style above, one sentence per paragraph, and additionally hard-wrap at 80 characters.

Always commit a Python file immediately after writing or modifying it — writing the code and committing it are one unit of work.

Never hardcode an API token, service account token, or any other secret into a committed file. Read the secret from wherever it already lives, at call time.

If a change is not in git, it does not exist. When `chezmoi apply` finds local drift, discard it with `chezmoi apply --force` rather than preserving it or asking whether to keep it.

Never run `chezmoi re-add` to capture drifted files into the dotfiles repo just to unblock an apply, because that commits edits that were never meant to be kept.
